// case: int-literal-out-of-range
// what: an integer literal is folded by `parseIntSelfhost`
//      (`compiler/builder.orb:199-221`), which accumulates `result * base +
//      digit` into an `int` and never checks the range. So a literal that does
//      not fit in the declared `int` is not an error, it is a different number:
//      2147483648 prints -2147483648 and 4294967296 prints 0. gcc rejects
//      `2147483648` for the same reason ("integer constant is so large that it
//      is unsigned"), and Python and C# reject it outright. A literal is not a
//      runtime value, so there is no later point at which this can be caught.
//      FINDINGS F-0014.
// known-defect: F-0014
// wrong-value: -2147483648\n0
fn main() -> int {
    print(2147483648)
    print(4294967296)
    return 0
}
